<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasco da Gama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1469–1524</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasco da Gama was a Portuguese navigator who led the first European voyage to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by sea, arriving at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in 1498 after rounding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. His voyage opened a direct maritime route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> treat it as foundational, permanently redirecting the spice trade away from overland routes and Venetian intermediaries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama.jpg" alt="Vasco da Gama" width="309" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ignoto_portoghese,_ritratto_di_un_cavaliere_dell%27ordine_di_cristo,_1525-50_ca._02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
